WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.818
NARRATOR: By the end of this lesson, you will
be able to trace one RISC-V ADD instruction

2
00:00:05.818 --> 00:00:10.229
from its thirty-two encoded bits to the value
written into a destination register.

3
00:00:10.229 --> 00:00:16.757
NARRATOR: You will also be able to explain why
a pipeline improves throughput, identify data

4
00:00:16.757 --> 00:00:20.674
and control hazards, and distinguish
architectural correctness from implementation

5
00:00:20.674 --> 00:00:21.109
speed.

6
00:00:21.109 --> 00:00:25.867
NARRATOR: Our worked instruction is add x5,
x6, x7.

7
00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:31.627
NARRATOR: We will use a classic five-stage
teaching pipeline: Instruction Fetch,

8
00:00:31.627 --> 00:00:35.816
Instruction Decode, Execute, Memory Access,
and Write Back.

9
00:00:35.816 --> 00:00:39.579
NARRATOR: That pipeline is not the only
possible processor design.

10
00:00:39.579 --> 00:00:44.841
NARRATOR: It is a clear model for seeing how
encoded instructions become coordinated data

11
00:00:44.841 --> 00:00:45.969
movement and control.

12
00:00:46.106 --> 00:00:49.155
NARRATOR: First, separate the contract from
the machinery.

13
00:00:49.155 --> 00:00:55.277
NARRATOR: The RISC-V ISA says what ADD means:
read two source registers, add their values,

14
00:00:55.277 --> 00:00:59.359
and write the low XLEN bits into the
destination register.

15
00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:04.117
NARRATOR: It also specifies the instruction
fields that identify that operation.

16
00:01:04.117 --> 00:01:06.158
NARRATOR: Software depends on this promise.

17
00:01:06.158 --> 00:01:10.957
NARRATOR: Internally, one processor might
execute an instruction in several simple

18
00:01:10.957 --> 00:01:16.193
stages, another might issue multiple
instructions at once, and a small controller

19
00:01:16.193 --> 00:01:18.811
might reuse hardware across many cycles.

20
00:01:18.811 --> 00:01:24.159
NARRATOR: Those designs may look very
different, yet a correct implementation must

21
00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:26.387
produce the same software-visible result.

22
00:01:26.387 --> 00:01:31.859
NARRATOR: This distinction keeps our reasoning
disciplined: begin with what must happen, then

23
00:01:31.859 --> 00:01:34.805
study how one pipeline makes it happen.

24
00:01:34.946 --> 00:01:38.454
NARRATOR: The processor receives bits, not
assembly text.

25
00:01:38.454 --> 00:01:45.661
NARRATOR: Our instruction word is hexadecimal
zero zero seven three zero two B three.

26
00:01:45.661 --> 00:01:52.627
NARRATOR: Reading the R-type fields from bit
thirty-one down to bit zero gives funct seven,

27
00:01:52.627 --> 00:01:55.878
rs2, rs1, funct three, rd, and opcode.

28
00:01:55.878 --> 00:02:00.597
NARRATOR: The opcode says this is a
register-register arithmetic instruction.

29
00:02:00.597 --> 00:02:04.819
NARRATOR: Funct three and funct seven narrow
that family to ADD.

30
00:02:04.819 --> 00:02:10.444
NARRATOR: The rs1 field is binary zero zero
one one zero, which names x6.

31
00:02:10.444 --> 00:02:13.582
NARRATOR: The rs2 field names x7.

32
00:02:13.582 --> 00:02:16.209
NARRATOR: The rd field names x5.

33
00:02:16.209 --> 00:02:21.526
NARRATOR: Decode therefore extracts both the
operation and the register addresses from one

34
00:02:21.526 --> 00:02:22.344
fixed-width word.

35
00:02:22.344 --> 00:02:26.951
NARRATOR: The assembly form is a
human-readable rendering of those exact

36
00:02:26.951 --> 00:02:27.370
fields.

37
00:02:27.506 --> 00:02:31.767
NARRATOR: Assume the program counter contains
zero x one thousand.

38
00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:36.507
NARRATOR: Fetch presents that address to
instruction memory and receives the

39
00:02:36.507 --> 00:02:37.800
thirty-two-bit ADD word.

40
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:43.675
NARRATOR: At the same time, the processor
forms the sequential next address, zero x one

41
00:02:43.675 --> 00:02:44.458
thousand four.

42
00:02:44.458 --> 00:02:50.921
NARRATOR: That plus-four step is valid for
this RV32I teaching example because we are not

43
00:02:50.921 --> 00:02:52.213
using compressed instructions.

44
00:02:52.213 --> 00:02:56.907
NARRATOR: Fetch does not yet know whether
control flow will remain sequential.

45
00:02:56.907 --> 00:03:00.925
NARRATOR: A branch or jump may later redirect
the program counter.

46
00:03:00.925 --> 00:03:06.006
NARRATOR: For now, the current address,
instruction bits, and predicted next address

47
00:03:06.006 --> 00:03:09.394
must remain aligned as the instruction moves
forward.

48
00:03:09.394 --> 00:03:13.892
NARRATOR: Pipeline state records the
information the Decode stage will need on the

49
00:03:13.892 --> 00:03:14.930
next clock cycle.

50
00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:19.783
NARRATOR: In Decode, the instruction fields
drive two related jobs.

51
00:03:19.783 --> 00:03:25.293
NARRATOR: First, the register file uses rs1
and rs2 as read addresses.

52
00:03:25.293 --> 00:03:31.428
NARRATOR: If x6 contains nineteen and x7
contains twenty-three, those values appear on

53
00:03:31.428 --> 00:03:33.316
the two read ports.

54
00:03:33.316 --> 00:03:38.329
NARRATOR: Second, control logic interprets the
opcode and function fields.

55
00:03:38.329 --> 00:03:43.542
NARRATOR: It selects an integer addition,
chooses two register operands for the ALU,

56
00:03:43.542 --> 00:03:48.354
disables a data-memory write, and enables a
later register write to x5.

57
00:03:48.354 --> 00:03:50.765
NARRATOR: Data alone is not enough.

58
00:03:50.765 --> 00:03:56.412
NARRATOR: The destination register number and
every control decision needed by later stages

59
00:03:56.412 --> 00:03:58.584
must advance beside the operands.

60
00:03:58.584 --> 00:04:03.374
NARRATOR: If a value and its control signal
become misaligned, the processor could compute

61
00:04:03.374 --> 00:04:06.454
correctly but write the result to the wrong
place.

62
00:04:06.602 --> 00:04:12.585
NARRATOR: The Execute stage supplies nineteen
and twenty-three to the arithmetic logic unit.

63
00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:17.898
NARRATOR: Operand-select controls choose the
two register values, and the ALU control

64
00:04:17.898 --> 00:04:18.784
selects addition.

65
00:04:18.784 --> 00:04:20.608
NARRATOR: The result is forty-two.

66
00:04:20.608 --> 00:04:28.284
NARRATOR: In RV32I, XLEN is thirty-two, so ADD
retains the low thirty-two bits of the

67
00:04:28.284 --> 00:04:29.307
mathematical sum.

68
00:04:29.307 --> 00:04:34.599
NARRATOR: If the signed mathematical result
exceeds the signed range, ADD still produces

69
00:04:34.599 --> 00:04:38.669
the low-bit result; it does not raise an
arithmetic-overflow exception.

70
00:04:38.669 --> 00:04:44.728
NARRATOR: The ISA defines that behavior, while
the ALU and surrounding control implement it.

71
00:04:44.728 --> 00:04:50.191
NARRATOR: For this example there is no
overflow, so the value forty-two and the

72
00:04:50.191 --> 00:04:53.312
destination number x5 advance toward the next
stage.

73
00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:56.819
NARRATOR: ADD does not need a data-memory read
or write.

74
00:04:56.819 --> 00:05:02.984
NARRATOR: Even so, in this regular five-stage
organization it occupies the Memory stage

75
00:05:02.984 --> 00:05:09.623
while its ALU result, destination register,
and write-enable bit move through the EX slash

76
00:05:09.623 --> 00:05:12.942
MEM and MEM slash WB pipeline registers.

77
00:05:12.942 --> 00:05:17.575
NARRATOR: A load instruction would use this
stage to read data, and a store would write

78
00:05:17.575 --> 00:05:17.865
data.

79
00:05:17.865 --> 00:05:20.659
NARRATOR: ADD simply preserves its result.

80
00:05:20.659 --> 00:05:26.338
NARRATOR: In Write Back, a result-select
control chooses the ALU value rather than

81
00:05:26.338 --> 00:05:31.143
memory data, the write-enable signal is
asserted, and x5 receives forty-two.

82
00:05:31.143 --> 00:05:35.830
NARRATOR: At that moment the instruction's
promised architectural effect becomes visible

83
00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:37.534
in the register file.

84
00:05:37.658 --> 00:05:39.738
NARRATOR: Why split the work into stages?

85
00:05:39.738 --> 00:05:44.623
NARRATOR: Because different instructions can
use different hardware during the same cycle.

86
00:05:44.623 --> 00:05:49.954
NARRATOR: While our ADD is in Execute, a
following independent instruction can be in

87
00:05:49.954 --> 00:05:52.620
Decode and another can be in Fetch.

88
00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:55.643
NARRATOR: During the next cycle, all three
advance.

89
00:05:55.643 --> 00:06:01.233
NARRATOR: After the pipeline fills, this ideal
sequence can complete roughly one instruction

90
00:06:01.233 --> 00:06:07.254
per cycle even though each instruction spends
several cycles traveling from Fetch to Write

91
00:06:07.254 --> 00:06:07.684
Back.

92
00:06:07.684 --> 00:06:12.741
NARRATOR: That is a throughput improvement,
not proof that one instruction has lower

93
00:06:12.741 --> 00:06:13.130
latency.

94
00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:18.364
NARRATOR: The clock period must accommodate
the slowest stage plus pipeline-register

95
00:06:18.364 --> 00:06:23.598
overhead, and the ideal completion rate
assumes instructions can advance without

96
00:06:23.598 --> 00:06:24.074
interruption.

97
00:06:24.218 --> 00:06:29.703
NARRATOR: Now place sub x8, x5, x9 immediately
after the ADD.

98
00:06:29.703 --> 00:06:35.552
NARRATOR: The subtraction needs the new x5 as
an input, but ordinary register-file timing

99
00:06:35.552 --> 00:06:39.729
may not provide that value when the
subtraction reaches Execute.

100
00:06:39.729 --> 00:06:44.906
NARRATOR: This is a read-after-write hazard:
the later instruction reads a register that

101
00:06:44.906 --> 00:06:46.897
the earlier instruction will write.

102
00:06:46.897 --> 00:06:51.725
NARRATOR: Hazard logic compares the source
registers of younger instructions with

103
00:06:51.725 --> 00:06:54.359
destination registers already in the pipeline.

104
00:06:54.359 --> 00:06:57.522
NARRATOR: Detecting the name match is only the
first step.

105
00:06:57.522 --> 00:07:02.341
NARRATOR: The processor must also know whether
the value is ready and where it currently

106
00:07:02.341 --> 00:07:02.662
resides.

107
00:07:02.662 --> 00:07:07.924
NARRATOR: Correctness requires the subtraction
to use forty-two, not the stale value

108
00:07:07.924 --> 00:07:09.678
previously stored in x5.

109
00:07:09.818 --> 00:07:15.025
NARRATOR: For an ALU-to-ALU dependency,
forwarding can often avoid a full wait for

110
00:07:15.025 --> 00:07:15.826
Write Back.

111
00:07:15.826 --> 00:07:22.786
NARRATOR: A bypass path routes the ADD result
from a later pipeline register directly to the

112
00:07:22.786 --> 00:07:24.091
subtraction's ALU input.

113
00:07:24.091 --> 00:07:29.909
NARRATOR: The register file may still contain
the old x5, but the operand multiplexer

114
00:07:29.909 --> 00:07:31.987
selects the newer forwarded value.

115
00:07:31.987 --> 00:07:35.074
NARRATOR: Forwarding does not solve every
dependency.

116
00:07:35.074 --> 00:07:40.732
NARRATOR: If a load produces data only after
Memory Access and the next instruction needs

117
00:07:40.732 --> 00:07:45.635
it immediately, the value may arrive too late
for the dependent Execute stage.

118
00:07:45.635 --> 00:07:50.405
NARRATOR: Hazard control then freezes selected
pipeline state and inserts a bubble.

119
00:07:50.405 --> 00:07:55.048
NARRATOR: One cycle of lost throughput is
preferable to an incorrect result.

120
00:07:55.154 --> 00:07:57.693
NARRATOR: Branches create a different
uncertainty.

121
00:07:57.693 --> 00:08:03.140
NARRATOR: Fetch wants a next address every
cycle, but the branch decision and target may

122
00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:04.593
not be known immediately.

123
00:08:04.593 --> 00:08:06.507
NARRATOR: A simple processor can wait.

124
00:08:06.507 --> 00:08:10.614
NARRATOR: A faster design may predict a
direction and continue fetching.

125
00:08:10.614 --> 00:08:16.282
NARRATOR: If the prediction is wrong, the
processor redirects the program counter and

126
00:08:16.282 --> 00:08:20.206
flushes younger wrong-path instructions before
they change architectural state.

127
00:08:20.206 --> 00:08:23.853
NARRATOR: A flush is not merely erasing a
diagram box.

128
00:08:23.853 --> 00:08:28.371
NARRATOR: It disables the invalid
instructions' control effects so they cannot

129
00:08:28.371 --> 00:08:30.014
write registers or memory.

130
00:08:30.014 --> 00:08:34.776
NARRATOR: Prediction can improve average
performance, but recovery logic preserves

131
00:08:34.776 --> 00:08:37.157
correctness when speculation is wrong.

132
00:08:37.298 --> 00:08:40.054
NARRATOR: Performance language needs
precision.

133
00:08:40.054 --> 00:08:45.039
NARRATOR: In an idealized scalar pipeline, the
steady-state cycles per instruction can

134
00:08:45.039 --> 00:08:45.870
approach one.

135
00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:51.655
NARRATOR: Real execution includes pipeline
filling and draining, data stalls, control

136
00:08:51.655 --> 00:08:54.811
redirects, cache delays, and other
constraints.

137
00:08:54.811 --> 00:09:00.723
NARRATOR: A useful first model is actual CPI
equals the ideal baseline plus the average

138
00:09:00.723 --> 00:09:02.299
stall cost per instruction.

139
00:09:02.299 --> 00:09:08.504
NARRATOR: This is not a universal performance
equation for every processor; it is a teaching

140
00:09:08.504 --> 00:09:08.918
decomposition.

141
00:09:08.918 --> 00:09:10.844
NARRATOR: Clock rate also matters.

142
00:09:10.844 --> 00:09:17.146
NARRATOR: Deeper pipelines can shorten some
stage delays, but they add registers, control

143
00:09:17.146 --> 00:09:20.054
complexity, and potentially larger recovery
penalties.

144
00:09:20.054 --> 00:09:25.175
NARRATOR: Architecture evaluation therefore
considers both work completed per cycle and

145
00:09:25.175 --> 00:09:27.503
the duration of each cycle.

146
00:09:27.626 --> 00:09:34.068
NARRATOR: We began with the word zero x zero
zero seven three zero two B three.

147
00:09:34.068 --> 00:09:42.257
NARRATOR: Decode identified ADD, rs1 x6, rs2
x7, and destination x5.

148
00:09:42.257 --> 00:09:46.032
NARRATOR: Fetch read the instruction at zero x
one thousand.

149
00:09:46.032 --> 00:09:49.093
NARRATOR: Decode read nineteen and
twenty-three.

150
00:09:49.093 --> 00:09:51.262
NARRATOR: Execute produced forty-two.

151
00:09:51.262 --> 00:09:55.075
NARRATOR: Memory Access performed no
data-memory operation.

152
00:09:55.075 --> 00:09:58.009
NARRATOR: Write Back committed forty-two to
x5.

153
00:09:58.009 --> 00:10:03.159
NARRATOR: Independent instructions could
overlap, while dependencies or branches

154
00:10:03.159 --> 00:10:06.593
required forwarding, stalls, redirects, or
flushing.

155
00:10:06.593 --> 00:10:11.185
NARRATOR: To test your understanding, ask four
questions about any instruction: What

156
00:10:11.185 --> 00:10:13.098
operation do the bits encode?

157
00:10:13.098 --> 00:10:15.496
NARRATOR: Which values enter each stage?

158
00:10:15.496 --> 00:10:18.213
NARRATOR: Which control signals must travel
with them?

159
00:10:18.213 --> 00:10:23.162
NARRATOR: And can any younger instruction
proceed without violating the ISA result?

160
00:10:23.162 --> 00:10:28.197
NARRATOR: If you can answer those, you can
reason about the pipeline rather than merely

161
00:10:28.197 --> 00:10:29.540
memorize its five boxes.
